    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Pressure solution is a physico-chemical process causing permanent deformation in rocks. This phenomenon has been widely studied by different methods over the past few decades. Analytical and numerical studies facilitate understanding the steps through which pressure solutions are formed. Pressure solution occur through three main steps:1) dissolution of solid material, 2) diffusion of dissolved material, 3) precipitation of dissolved material. Each of these steps is governed by certain kinetic laws some of which are empirical.\r\n\r\nIn this study the kinetics of pressure solution are reviewed and some of the models in the literature used to simulate this process are compared. According to these comparisons thin film model which is the simplest model to simulate the pressure solution might not give the best result, especially for larger grain diameters. Also, effect of some parameters such as stress on the governing factors like solubility or diffusion of materials should be further studied to choose the most suitable constitutive law in each case.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Rahmani, Heila, Aydin, Atilla and Borja, Ronaldo. (2009). A Review of Kinetics of Pressure Solution. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/zj887gs9613",
